    rdf:value """the art of the un-aesthetic; but taken consciously as subjective it is perhaps the highest form of beauty. 
By the way, it is very “half educated” to distinguish between active and contemplative life. Without either the other perishes. So let us throw stones at neither”.
————
We had an enchanting walk to our Tree, the air and sunshine perfect – “A day in April never came so sweet.” Coming back we found Miss Taylor and her deadly, deadly parents, who paid an unduly long call. Worked in the evening. 
* Thursday. April 8. 97. Villa Rosa. Fiesole
Bernhard went with Horne to the drawings, lunched with him, then to the Corsini and Palazzo Vecchio, and then to call on Benn. """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 110 ;
